# aarch64 under QEMU `virt`, reading the PCI Express host bridge the device tree describes.
#
# The same kernel as aarch64-virt, with PCIE on. `virt` always has a
# `pci-host-ecam-generic` node; nothing binds it today, so the kernel walks past it. With
# this on, the kernel reads what the tree says about it -- where configuration space is,
# which buses are behind the bridge, the windows it forwards, and the controller its
# messages are mapped to -- and checks that description for the coherence an enumerator
# would depend on.
#
# Reading only. No window is claimed, no register is touched, and no bus is enumerated,
# because on this port configuration space is not addressable when discovery runs: the boot
# tables map two gigabytes and the window sits a quarter of a terabyte up. The PCs do not
# have that problem, which is why they enumerate during discovery and this does not.
#
# This is the first step toward a disk on PCIe, which would be the first device on this port
# whose DMA an SMMUv3 could translate -- the unit is wired to the PCIe root complex alone,
# and every device here today is a memory-mapped virtio slot outside it. See
# docs/isolation.md.
ARCH_AARCH64=y
SMP=n
EARLY_CONSOLE=y
DEBUG_BUILD=y
QEMU_EXIT=y
PCIE=y
QEMU_PCIE_BLOCK=y
SIZE_BUDGET_KIB=2048
